    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xy090j4",
      "content": "Human physiology is the study of functions of the human body and how different organs and systems work together to maintain life. In nursing competitive examinations such as RRB Nursing, questions are commonly asked about blood composition, cardiac cycle, respiration, digestion, kidney function, hormones, and normal physiological values."
    },
    {
      "id": "i6th564",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Cell Physiology"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "oldiyb0",
      "content": "Cells are the basic functional units of the body. Cell physiology includes metabolism, transport mechanisms, and cellular communication."
    },
    {
      "id": "cdw6gw4",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Cell Process</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Diffusion</p>",
            "<p>Movement of molecules from higher concentration to lower concentration</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Osmosis</p>",
            "<p>Movement of water across a semipermeable membrane</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Active Transport</p>",
            "<p>Movement of substances against concentration gradient using ATP</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Facilitated Diffusion</p>",
            "<p>Transport with help of carrier proteins</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Endocytosis</p>",
            "<p>Cell engulfs particles</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Exocytosis</p>",
            "<p>Cell releases substances outside</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kta5xa9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Blood Physiology"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "yy4xp1s",
      "content": "Blood is a connective tissue responsible for transport of oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and waste products."
    },
    {
      "id": "tay4sb4",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Component</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Function</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Red Blood Cells (RBC)</p>",
            "<p>Carry oxygen using hemoglobin</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>White Blood Cells (WBC)</p>",
            "<p>Defense against infection</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Platelets</p>",
            "<p>Blood clotting</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Plasma</p>",
            "<p>Liquid part carrying nutrients and hormones</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kxiob4m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Normal Blood Values (Important Exam Facts)"
    },
    {
      "id": "o5y27mr",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Parameter</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Normal Value</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hemoglobin (Male)</p>",
            "<p>13–17 g/dL</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hemoglobin (Female)</p>",
            "<p>12–15 g/dL</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>RBC Count</p>",
            "<p>4.5–6 million cells/mm³</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>WBC Count</p>",
            "<p>4,000–11,000 cells/mm³</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Platelet Count</p>",
            "<p>150,000–450,000/mm³</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Blood pH</p>",
            "<p>7.35 – 7.45</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fs0sk0b",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Cardiac Physiology"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ocylkl1",
      "content": "The heart pumps blood throughout the body. The cardiac cycle includes systole (contraction) and diastole (relaxation)."
    },
    {
      "id": "hs8akjk",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Term</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Meaning</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Systole</p>",
            "<p>Contraction phase of heart</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Diastole</p>",
            "<p>Relaxation phase</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Stroke Volume</p>",
            "<p>Amount of blood pumped per beat</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Cardiac Output</p>",
            "<p>Heart rate × Stroke volume</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
